--TEST--
An `#[Access]` on a method that declares no route is refused rather than ignored
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `#[Access]` is the required sibling of `#[Route]`, and the refusal a
// route carrying no decision gets is that sentence read in one direction. This
// is the other one: a decision guarding no route. Nothing can come along later
// and give it a meaning, because the compiler promises that it never
// interprets what `allow` names — the route table is the only reader there is.
class Users {
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function index(): string { return "u"; }
}

// The same attribute on a method that does carry a `#[Route]` is `rule:attributes/access-is-a-required-sibling`'s ordinary
// case, and the payload is written identically — which is what makes the
// refusal above a fact about the missing sibling rather than about the decision.
class Search {
    #[Core\Route(path: "/search", method: Core\Http\Method::Get)]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function index(): string { return "s"; }
}

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0788]: this `#[Access]` guards no route
  --> case.nvs:8:7
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
